# Wrapped operators that do not yet state the operands they take.
#
# An operator installed by wrapping a procedure registers no signature
# unless it is given one, so the dispatcher cannot check its operands
# and the body must. Each name here is one that still checks for itself,
# or does not check at all.
#
# The list only shrinks. tests/check-op-signatures.sh fails on a wrapped
# operator that is neither listed here nor stating its operands, and on
# an entry here that has since gained a statement.
#
# The names below take operands a single statement cannot describe:
#   colorimage imagemask                a source that is one string or
#                                       one per colour component
#   rectclip rectfill rectstroke        four numbers, an array, or an
#                                       encoded string
#   infill ineofill instroke            a point or a user path
#   inufill inueofill inustroke
#   setcolor setpattern                 components whose number and kind
#                                       follow the current colour space
#   setscreen                           a spot function given or named,
#                                       sampled against the caller's stack
#   setcolorscreen setcachedevice2      more operands than a statement
#                                       may carry
# Each checks its own operands. Giving them statements needs an operator
# to carry more than one, the way the C side already does.
#
# flattenpath and pathbbox are named in the wrapping list but are not
# defined in PostScript at all: the loop wraps only what it finds in
# userdict, so it passes over them and they stay the C operators they
# always were, with the operand types C declares for them.
colorimage
imagemask
ineofill
infill
instroke
inueofill
inufill
inustroke
rectclip
rectfill
rectstroke
setcachedevice2
setcolor
setcolorscreen
setpattern
setscreen
flattenpath
pathbbox
